<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Dostoevsky is a 43-year-old man with epilepsy on lamotrigine 100 mg twice daily and with gambling disorder in early recovery, who called the nurse line this morning and was seen the same day. Thirty-six hours ago he developed nausea and vomiting, followed by watery diarrhea about six times a day, after a dinner with friends, two of whom have had the same illness. He vomited his evening lamotrigine dose within half an hour two nights ago, did not take yesterday morning&apos;s dose because he could not keep water down, and kept down last night&apos;s dose. He has had no seizure, no warning symptoms, and no rash. There is no blood in the stool, no high fever, and no severe abdominal pain. He has been sipping an oral rehydration drink since last night and has kept it down. He slept poorly with the illness and is worried that the missed doses and the lost sleep will bring on a seizure. He has not gambled. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Thirty-six hours ago</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Upper abdomen and bowel</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>A day and a half</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Nausea, vomiting, and watery diarrhea</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; vomiting has stopped since last night</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Diarrhea about six times a day</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Two friends from the same dinner have the same illness</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Oral rehydration drink kept down since last night</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>Two lamotrigine doses missed or vomited; no seizure, rash, blood in the stool, or high fever</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

