  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral gastroenteritis with mild dehydration (A08.4)</content>. Likely norovirus, given the shared meal and the short illness in others. Mild dehydration, with normal sodium 137, potassium 3.6, creatinine 1.08, and glucose 84. No features that call for stool testing or antibiotics. [S]<list><item>Oral rehydration solution in small, frequent sips, then a normal diet as tolerated.</item><item>Ondansetron is not prescribed, because vomiting has stopped.</item><item>Hand washing and cleaning of surfaces with a bleach-based product; he should not prepare food for others until two days after the symptoms stop.</item><item>Return or call the same day for blood in the stool, inability to keep fluids down, fainting, or very little urine.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Epilepsy on lamotrigine, two missed doses (G40.909)</content>. Two missed doses and poor sleep raise the risk of a breakthrough seizure. He kept down last night&apos;s dose. Lamotrigine has a long half-life, so a single extra catch-up dose is not needed now and doubling doses is avoided. [S]<list><item>Take this morning&apos;s dose now and continue 100 mg twice daily.</item><item>If a dose is vomited within about an hour, take it again once the vomiting has settled; call if two doses in a row cannot be kept down, because a temporary rescue plan would then be considered with neurology.</item><item>Seizure precautions while unwell: no baths, no cooking alone at the stove, and a friend to check in by phone this evening and tomorrow morning.</item><item>Prioritize sleep for the next two nights; no writing through the night.</item><item>Record the missed doses in his seizure diary.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gambling disorder, early recovery (F63.0)</content>. No gambling. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline is in his phone. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly cognitive behavioral therapy; reschedule this week&apos;s session by phone.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guideline update summary: Efficacy and tolerability of the new antiepileptic drugs I: Treatment of new-onset epilepsy (2018); 2017 Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Infectious Diarrhea (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse telephone check tomorrow. Four-month chronic-care visit in four weeks as scheduled. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-07: four-month review of seizure control, mood, and gambling recovery. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
